<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hussein Kamel of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1853–1917</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Hussein Kamel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hussein Kamel was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a> and uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in December 1914, deposing Abbas II and ending Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, it made Hussein Kamel ruler with the title of sultan, marking the break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. He reigned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, when Egypt served as a major British base, and was succeeded on his death in 1917 by his brother Fuad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussein-kamel-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Hussein Kamel of Egypt" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Eastman House from Rochester, NY, United States · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch._Chusseau-Flaviens_-_George_Eastman_House_-_Egypt-Prince_Hussein_Pacha_(Khedive_)_(pd).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
